XYDown was written to provide easy file downloads from inside ofEddie Kuns' KBCom terminal program.  It is a stand-alone programwhich will automatically sense whether the sender is usingXModem, YModem, or YModem-Batch, and will adjust accordingly.It will also perform end-of-line conversions on received textfiles, and, if no file type is specified on the command line,it will attempt to automatically decide the file type based onthe first 1k or so of the file.   One note:  If you are on a system where memory is tight,or where disk I/O is fairly slow (i.e. on a floppy-basedsystem), then you may have problems with certain hoststhat have stringent timing requirements (I've run intothis with "sb" on Unix systems).  In that case, reducethe size of BUFFSIZE in the file disk.c and recompile.This is currently set to 15000 bytes for OSk systemsor 1500 bytes for OS9/6809 systems.This text file is a brief discussion of the source code forXYDown.The source code consists of a number of C files.  To compile,first compile each C source file to a .r relocatable file,for example:    cc  -r serial.cThen, compile all of the .r files together into one executablefile, i.e.    cc serial.r crc.r term.r xydown.r disk.r -f=xydownNote: the crc.c file contains two versions of the crc calculatingcode.  To compile the assembly language version (for OS9/6809only), use the command   cc crc.c -r -dm6809A makefile is enclosed to automate this process for people witha make command.XYDown was written with portability in mind.  It should be easilyadaptable to most situations.  The following description of thesource files should help:term.c   -- This file contains routines which display screen output,   including error and status messages.  Since all screen output   is done through these routines, it can easily be adapted to   any particular type of display.crc.c   -- This contains routines which calculate the necessary   16-bit CRC.  There should be no need to edit this file.disk.c  -- This controls disk output.  In particular, the   routine settype() does the automatic ASCII/Binary decision,   and can be modified if necessary.serial.c  -- This handles all serial output.  The routine   readchar() returns the next serial character, or -1 if a timeout   occurs.  readblock() is like readchar, but optimized for handling   large numbers of characters.xyprot.c  -- Handles the actual download protocol.xydown.c  -- This does command-line parsing, calls routines in other   source files to prepare the disk, terminal, and serial I/O,   and initiates the transfer.  Once the transfer is initiated,   control passes to xyprot.c